mod('signal','Cell signaling',
 'Cells talk to each other with chemical messages. The message never has to enter the cell: the receiving cell converts it into an internal signal.',
 ['Cell communication has three stages. In <b>reception</b>, a signaling molecule (<b>ligand</b>) binds a specific receptor protein, usually on the cell surface (small nonpolar signals such as steroid hormones can cross the membrane and bind receptors inside). In <b>transduction</b>, the receptor changes shape and starts a chain of molecules, often a <b>phosphorylation cascade</b> in which protein kinases activate one another. Second messengers such as cAMP and Ca²⁺ spread the signal quickly. In the <b>response</b>, the cell changes what it does: turns genes on or off, activates an enzyme, or reshapes its cytoskeleton.',
  'Cascades <b>amplify</b> the signal because each activated enzyme activates many molecules in the next step, so a few ligand molecules can trigger a huge response. Signals are <b>specific</b> because only cells with the right receptor respond. Signals act over different distances: direct contact (cell junctions), <b>paracrine</b> (local), <b>synaptic</b> (neurons) and <b>endocrine</b> (hormones in blood). A mutation in any step can block or constantly activate a pathway, which is a common cause of cancer.'],
 ('a message had to enter a cell to be received','that a receptor can turn an outside signal into an inside one','how a tiny signal causes a large response, and what goes wrong in disease','reception, transduction, response and amplification'),
 [('ligand + receptor → shape change','reception'),('kinase cascade: each step activates many molecules','transduction and amplification'),('response: gene expression, enzyme activity, movement','response'),('mutation can block or lock on a pathway','disease')],
 [stagelab('sg-lab','A signal pathway',canvas='sg-canvas',aria='A cell surface receptor binds a ligand, starts a phosphorylation cascade inside the cell and triggers a response.',p='sg',n=4),
  lab('sg-lab2','Amplification and mutations',canvas='sg-canvas2',aria='Bars showing how many molecules are active at each step of a three-step cascade.',
   seg=('sg-mut','Cell',[('norm','normal'),('blk','receptor cannot bind'),('stk','receptor stuck "on"')]),
   ctl=[('sg-n','Ligand molecules',1,20,1,3),('sg-a','Amplification per step',2,50,1,10)],
   out=[('sg-end','active molecules at the end','x'),('sg-resp','response','')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the cascade stage. Why does each step increase the signal? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#sg-st",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Each activated kinase phosphorylates many copies of the next protein, so the number of active molecules multiplies at each step.'),
  ('Choose "stuck on" with 1 ligand. What happens? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#sg-mut [data-k=stk]"],["#sg-n",1]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The pathway runs at full strength with no ligand: the cell responds as if the signal were always present, a typical mechanism of cancer.')],
 ('A signal activates a receptor. The receptor activates 20 kinase A molecules; each kinase A activates 20 kinase B molecules; each kinase B activates 20 enzymes. How many active enzymes result from 1 ligand-bound receptor?',[
  '<b>Step 1.</b> 1 receptor → 20 kinase A.',
  '<b>Step 2.</b> 20 × 20 = 400 kinase B.',
  '<b>Step 3.</b> 400 × 20 = 8,000 active enzymes.',
  '<b>Meaning.</b> One signaling event becomes a response of thousands of molecules: amplification.']),
 ['Saying the ligand enters the cell and causes the response directly (true only for small nonpolar ligands, and then it binds an internal receptor).','Thinking the signal is amplified at reception only.','Forgetting that specificity comes from the receptor.','Calling a response "transduction". Transduction is the relay.','Believing that more ligand always gives a bigger response (receptors can saturate).'],
 ('Predicting what a mutation does',['Name the step affected. If the receptor cannot bind, no response. If a kinase is stuck on, a response without signal. If the cascade is blocked downstream, the ligand binds but nothing happens. Describe the effect on the response and on the cell.']),
 [('A cell lacks the receptor for a hormone. What is the most likely result when the hormone is present?',['no response from that cell','a response in all nearby cells','a stronger response','the cell makes more hormone'],0,'Without the specific receptor the cell cannot detect the signal.')])

mod('feedback','Feedback & homeostasis',
 'Living things keep conditions steady by measuring them and correcting any drift. Occasionally they do the opposite on purpose.',
 ['<b>Negative feedback</b> reverses a change and brings a variable back to its <b>set point</b>: body temperature, blood glucose, blood pH and water balance. A sensor detects a change, a control center compares it with the set point, and an effector acts to oppose it. For blood glucose, high glucose makes the pancreas release <b>insulin</b> (cells take up glucose, liver stores glycogen); low glucose makes it release <b>glucagon</b> (liver releases glucose).',
  '<b>Positive feedback</b> amplifies the change and pushes the system away from where it started until something ends it. Examples: oxytocin and uterine contractions during childbirth, ripening fruit releasing ethylene that speeds ripening, and blood clotting. Positive feedback is not a failure of homeostasis; it drives a process to completion. Without any feedback a variable drifts toward the surroundings.'],
 ('a system just responded to conditions as they were','that outputs can feed back to change the process','how organisms stay stable, and how some processes snowball','negative and positive feedback loops'),
 [('negative feedback: change → response that opposes it','stabilizes'),('positive feedback: change → response that amplifies it','drives to completion'),('sensor → control center → effector','loop parts'),('insulin lowers blood glucose; glucagon raises it','example of negative feedback')],
 [lab('fb-lab','Keep the temperature steady',canvas='fb-canvas',aria='A graph of a variable over time with a dashed set point line. The behaviour changes with the type of feedback and the surrounding temperature.',
   seg=('fb-kind','Feedback',[('none','none'),('neg','negative'),('pos','positive')]),
   ctl=[('fb-amb','Surrounding temperature',0,40,1,15)],
   pre='<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn" id="fb-reset">Restart</button></div>',
   out=[('fb-now','current value','x'),('fb-dev','difference from set point (37)','a'),('fb-msg','what is happening','')],own=True),
  stagelab('fb-lab2','Blood glucose after a meal',canvas='fb-canvas2',aria='A graph of blood glucose over time with the hormone response at each stage.',p='bg',n=4)],
 [('Choose negative feedback with the surroundings at 15 °C. What happens to the value? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#fb-kind [data-k=neg]"],["#fb-amb",15]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','It settles near the set point despite the cold: the effector (heat production) opposes the drift.'),
  ('Choose positive feedback. What happens? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#fb-kind [data-k=pos]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The value runs away from the set point (up or down), because each change makes the next change bigger.')],
 ('Blood glucose rises after a meal. Describe the feedback loop that returns it to normal.',[
  '<b>Sensor.</b> Pancreatic beta cells detect high blood glucose.',
  '<b>Response.</b> They release insulin into the blood.',
  '<b>Effectors.</b> Body cells take up glucose and the liver converts it to glycogen.',
  '<b>Result.</b> Blood glucose falls toward the set point and insulin release decreases: negative feedback.']),
 ['Calling negative feedback "bad". It means the response opposes the change.','Thinking positive feedback means a good result.','Saying positive feedback maintains homeostasis. It amplifies a change.','Forgetting that insulin and glucagon act oppositely.','Not identifying the effector.'],
 ('Naming the loop on the exam',['Say what changed, what detected it, what responded, and whether the response reduced the change (negative) or increased it (positive). Label examples: temperature, glucose, blood pressure (negative); childbirth, fruit ripening, clotting (positive).']),
 [('The release of oxytocin during childbirth that increases contractions is an example of',['positive feedback','negative feedback','a set point','a receptor'],0,'The response (contractions) causes more oxytocin release, amplifying the change.')])

mod('cellcycle','The cell cycle & mitosis',
 'Before a cell divides it has to copy all of its DNA and then split the copies evenly. Mitosis is the splitting.',
 ['The cycle has two main parts. <b>Interphase</b> (most of the time) includes G₁ (growth), S (DNA is replicated, so each chromosome becomes two identical <b>sister chromatids</b>) and G₂ (more growth and preparation). <b>M phase</b> is mitosis followed by <b>cytokinesis</b> (division of the cytoplasm).',
  'Mitosis has four stages. <b>Prophase</b>: chromosomes condense, the spindle forms and the nuclear envelope breaks down. <b>Metaphase</b>: chromosomes line up at the middle. <b>Anaphase</b>: sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles. <b>Telophase</b>: nuclei re-form and the chromosomes relax. The two daughter cells are genetically identical to the parent and to each other. Mitosis is used for growth, repair and (in many organisms) asexual reproduction. In animal cells cytokinesis pinches the cell (cleavage furrow); in plant cells a cell plate forms.'],
 ('cells just divided in two','that the DNA must first be copied and then separated exactly','why daughter cells are identical copies','phases of interphase and mitosis'),
 [('G₁ → S → G₂ → M','cell cycle order'),('prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase','PMAT'),('DNA content: 2C → 4C in S → 2C after division','per cell'),('fraction of cells in a stage ≈ fraction of time','used in root-tip counts')],
 [stagelab('cc-lab','Phases of the cell cycle',canvas='cc-canvas',aria='A cell with four chromosomes drawn at each stage of interphase and mitosis, with the DNA amount shown.',p='cc',n=7,out_extra=[('cc-dna','DNA per cell','a')]),
  lab('cc-lab2','Counting cells in a root tip',canvas='cc-canvas2',aria='Bars showing the hours a cell spends in each stage, calculated from counts of cells in each stage.',
   ctl=[('cc-i','Cells in interphase',0,200,1,142),('cc-p','Cells in prophase',0,100,1,22),('cc-m','Cells in metaphase',0,100,1,10),('cc-a','Cells in anaphase',0,100,1,8),('cc-t','Cells in telophase',0,100,1,18),('cc-d','Cell cycle duration (hours)',6,36,1,24)],
   out=[('cc-n','cells counted','x'),('cc-ih','interphase','a'),('cc-mh','mitosis in total','v')],own=True)],
 [('Go to anaphase. What separates, and what does each pole get? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cc-st",5]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Sister chromatids separate (each is now a chromosome) and each pole receives a complete set of chromosomes.'),
  ('Go to S phase. What happens to the DNA amount? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cc-st",1]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','It doubles (from 2C to 4C) as every chromosome is copied. The chromosome number stays the same until anaphase.')],
 ('In a root tip squash, 200 cells are counted: 160 in interphase, 20 in prophase, 8 in metaphase, 4 in anaphase and 8 in telophase. If the cell cycle takes 24 hours, how long is spent in interphase and in mitosis?',[
  '<b>Fractions.</b> Interphase 160/200 = 0.80; mitosis 40/200 = 0.20.',
  '<b>Interphase time.</b> 0.80 × 24 h = 19.2 h.',
  '<b>Mitosis time.</b> 0.20 × 24 h = 4.8 h.',
  '<b>Assumption.</b> Cells are evenly distributed through the cycle (an asynchronous population).']),
 ['Saying chromosomes double in number in S phase (the number of chromosomes stays the same; each now has two chromatids).','Mixing up the order of anaphase and metaphase.','Forgetting cytokinesis is separate from mitosis.','Saying daughter cells differ genetically.','Thinking DNA replication happens in prophase.'],
 ('Chromosomes and chromatids',['Count chromosomes by centromeres. A cell with 4 chromosomes has 8 chromatids after S phase until anaphase, when each chromatid is counted as a chromosome (so 8 chromosomes briefly). After cytokinesis each daughter has 4 chromosomes and no sister chromatids.']),
 [('During which stage do sister chromatids separate?',['anaphase','metaphase','prophase','S phase'],0,'Sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles in anaphase.')])

mod('cycleregs','Cell cycle control & cancer',
 'Cells do not divide just because they can. A series of checkpoints decides whether a cell should go ahead, and cancer is what happens when the checkpoints fail.',
 ['The cycle is controlled by <b>cyclins</b> and <b>cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs)</b>. A CDK is only active when bound to its cyclin, whose level rises and falls through the cycle. Three main <b>checkpoints</b> stop the cycle unless conditions are right: <b>G₁ checkpoint</b> (is the cell big enough, are nutrients and growth factors present, is the DNA undamaged?), <b>G₂ checkpoint</b> (has the DNA been copied accurately?) and the <b>M (spindle) checkpoint</b> (are all chromosomes attached to the spindle before anaphase?). Cells that stop can enter G₀ (a resting state).',
  'The protein <b>p53</b> halts the cycle when DNA is damaged and triggers repair, or <b>apoptosis</b> (programmed cell death) if the damage is too great. Cancer arises when mutations break this control. Mutations that make <b>proto-oncogenes</b> overactive (oncogenes) act like a stuck accelerator; mutations that disable <b>tumor suppressor genes</b> (such as p53) remove the brakes. Usually several mutations are needed. Cancer cells divide without signals and ignore apoptosis.'],
 ('division was simply on or off','that checkpoints check conditions and can pause or cancel division','how cancer can be explained as damaged control of the cell cycle','checkpoints, cyclins and CDKs, and oncogenes vs tumor suppressors'),
 [('cyclin + CDK → active kinase','drives the cell through a checkpoint'),('G₁, G₂, M checkpoints','conditions must be met to continue'),('proto-oncogene → oncogene','stuck accelerator'),('tumor suppressor (p53) lost','no brakes, no apoptosis')],
 [lab('cr-lab','Will the cell divide?',canvas='cr-canvas',aria='A loop of the cell cycle with three checkpoint gates. The cell proceeds or stops depending on the conditions you choose.',
   seg=('cr-gf','Growth factor',[('on','present'),('off','absent')]),
   pre='<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn" id="cr-dmg" aria-pressed="false">DNA damage: no</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="cr-p53" aria-pressed="true">p53: working</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="cr-spin" aria-pressed="true">Spindle attached: yes</button></div>',
   out=[('cr-res','outcome','x'),('cr-why','why','')],own=True),
  lab('cr-lab2','How fast can a tumor grow?',canvas='cr-canvas2',aria='Graph of the number of cells over time for the chosen doubling time, compared with a cell population that does not divide.',
   ctl=[('cr-dt','Doubling time (hours)',12,72,1,24),('cr-days','Time (days)',1,30,1,14)],
   out=[('cr-n','cells after that time','x'),('cr-x','increase','a')],own=True)],
 [('Turn on DNA damage while p53 is working. What happens? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#cr-dmg"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The G₁ checkpoint stops the cycle; p53 triggers repair and, if damage is too great, apoptosis.'),
  ('Now turn p53 off (mutated) while the DNA is damaged. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#cr-p53"]) + '\'>Turn p53 off</button>','','The cell passes the checkpoint with damaged DNA and divides, passing the damage on: a step toward cancer.')],
 ('A tumor cell population starts with 1 cell and doubles every 24 hours. How many cells after 10 days, assuming no cell death?',[
  '<b>Doublings.</b> 10 days × 24 h / 24 h = 10 doublings.',
  '<b>Cells.</b> 2<sup>10</sup> = 1,024 cells.',
  '<b>Note.</b> Real tumors slow down because of limited nutrients and blood supply.']),
 ['Saying the checkpoints are only in mitosis.','Thinking cancer cells divide faster because the cell cycle is shorter (the main problem is loss of control).','Confusing oncogenes (gain of function) with tumor suppressor mutations (loss of function).','Saying p53 causes cancer. Mutated p53 fails to stop it.','Thinking one mutation causes cancer.'],
 ('Explain-it template',['Pick the checkpoint, say what it normally checks, say what the mutation changes, and say the consequence: "A mutation that disables p53 means damaged DNA is not detected at the G₁ checkpoint, so the cell divides and passes on mutations."']),
 [('A mutation inactivates a tumor suppressor gene. The most likely effect is',['loss of a brake on cell division','a protein that is always active as an accelerator','faster apoptosis','shorter G₀'],0,'Tumor suppressor genes normally restrain division, so losing them removes a brake.')])
